WEBVTT

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.288
Hi there.

00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:14.128
Welcome to this video where we are going
to learn about disinformation.

00:00:14.816 --> 00:00:19.056
The goal of the video is to help you
to understand what disinformation is,

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:22.496
what motivates people to do it,
and how to recognize it.

00:00:23.008 --> 00:00:26.656
In the video, firstly,
we will look at what disinformation is.

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:30.192
Then we will look
at some examples of disinformation

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.232
and why it spreads so easily.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.928
What is disinformation?

00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:41.888
Disinformation is false
or misleading information

00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:44.880
that's deliberately created
and spread to deceive people.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.600
It is not just getting facts
wrong by accident,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.920
but purposefully creating or sharing
false information to manipulate,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.360
confuse, mislead, or even do some harm.

00:00:56.272 --> 00:00:58.512
This is different from misinformation,

00:00:58.512 --> 00:01:01.632
which is false information
spread without malicious intent.

00:01:01.744 --> 00:01:05.504
Someone might genuinely believe something
if is correct and share it.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.928
It is also different
from malinformation,

00:01:08.928 --> 00:01:12.288
which involves sharing 
genuine information but with the intent

00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:16.208
to cause harm, like leaking
private documents to embarrass someone.

00:01:21.872 --> 00:01:26.464
Some common examples of disinformation
include made up news stories,

00:01:27.232 --> 00:01:31.024
images or videos shared as authentic
but which have been changed (doctored),

00:01:31.152 --> 00:01:35.744
fake social media accounts
spreading propaganda,

00:01:36.544 --> 00:01:39.552
misleading statistics
taken out of context,

00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:42.944
conspiracy theories designed 
to sow confusion.

00:01:43.552 --> 00:01:46.544
All of them are shared
with a negative intention.

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:51.392
Fake news refers to any misleading
information claimed to be legitimate news,

00:01:51.392 --> 00:01:56.304
but actually spreading misinformation,
disinformation, propaganda and hoax.

00:01:56.864 --> 00:02:00.624
For example, someone posts 
a news article about a new movie sequel

00:02:00.624 --> 00:02:03.904
swith your favorite actor
that you were hoping would be made.

00:02:04.544 --> 00:02:07.552
However, it turns out to be just a hoax.

00:02:12.208 --> 00:02:16.096
Disinformation can spread rapidly,
especially on social media,

00:02:16.176 --> 00:02:19.936
because people often share content
without checking its accuracy.

00:02:20.608 --> 00:02:25.248
It can be about any topic health,
politics, science, current events,

00:02:25.248 --> 00:02:27.376
and can have real world consequences

00:02:27.376 --> 00:02:30.368
when people make decisions
based on false information.

00:02:31.088 --> 00:02:35.616
The challenge with disinformation is
that it's not always easy to identify,

00:02:35.808 --> 00:02:39.408
and correcting it can be difficult
once it's widely circulated.

00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:45.168
People tend to remember the initial 
false claim even after seeing a correction.

00:02:46.288 --> 00:02:49.376
Disinformation is often
created to exploit emotions

00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:52.816
like fear or outrage,
making it more likely to spread.

00:02:54.016 --> 00:02:58.176
Common goals behind disinformation
include influencing elections,

00:02:58.256 --> 00:03:01.616
undermining trust in businesses
such as game developers,

00:03:01.696 --> 00:03:06.096
or creating division
between people due to disinformation.

00:03:06.128 --> 00:03:10.048
School students can be tricked and lose
money or access to their accounts.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:14.176
The internet and social media
have made it both easier to create

00:03:14.176 --> 00:03:18.368
convincing disinformation and to 
spread it rapidly to large audiences.

00:03:19.056 --> 00:03:22.608
So in this short video, 
we learned what disinformation is 

00:03:22.608 --> 00:03:26.336
and looked at some examples 
of disinformation and why it spreads so easily.

00:03:27.328 --> 00:03:28.896
Thank you for watching.

00:03:28.896 --> 00:03:31.840
Do not forget to watch
other videos in our series.